# 11. CODE QUALITY AND MODULE RULES

A module is unacceptable when it becomes a new catch-all owner.

### Required

- Explicit responsibility.
- Cohesive functions.
- Typed public interfaces.
- Limited dependencies.
- No cyclic domain imports.
- No duplicated authoritative policy.
- No unrelated RPC collections.
- No hidden global mutable business store.
- No new feature-specific credentials.
- No new generic server-side authorization logic in React.
- No fabricated responses.
- No unnecessary dependencies.
- No wholesale formatting passes.

### Size discipline

Prefer small files that can be reviewed and tested independently.

A target of roughly 100–300 lines for focused presentation modules may be used as a review heuristic, **not as a hard product requirement**.

Larger modules must have an explicit cohesion justification.

A split is not accepted merely because an oversized file becomes shorter.

The actual test is whether the new boundary reflects one coherent responsibility with correct imports and consumers.

### Static dependency check

Create or use the existing appropriate dependency-boundary tests to reject:

- A domain importing another domain's private implementation.
- A UI module importing a server-only module.
- Parent Notes depending on Messaging's internal policy.
- Calendar owning class membership mutation.
- Attendance owning Academics membership mutation.
- Directory mutating private Teacher school records.
- Students creating a second pupil identity.
- Generic school modules collecting unrelated RPCs again.

Do not invent rules inconsistent with the approved architecture.

Record every boundary rule and its source-document authority.

